course: dictionary
lesson: 14794
title: Pulverised
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pulverised means crushed into powder or into tiny pieces. «The rock was pulverised into fine grey dust.» You can picture it: the rock was smashed until only powder was left.
explain: The word can also be used in sport, in a playful way. «Our team got pulverised 7–0.» Nobody was crushed into dust here. It means the team lost very heavily. This use is informal, so it is fine in a chatty report but less common in formal writing.
explain: Context clues help you work out the meaning. Look for words like dust, wrecking ball or 7–0. Then ask what would fit: crushed, destroyed or beaten. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: Pulverised means crushed into powder or tiny pieces.
fact: In sport, «pulverised» is informal and means beaten very heavily.
fact: «Pulverised» can also mean completely destroyed, as in «The old building was pulverised by the wrecking ball.»
fact: In American English the spelling is pulverized.
fact: Other forms of the word include pulverise, pulverises and pulverising.
exercise: «The rock was pulverised into fine grey dust.» What does pulverised mean here? | Polished until shiny | Carried far away | *Crushed into powder | Painted bright blue
exercise: Ella says «Our team got pulverised 7–0» means they had a lovely time with cake. What does it really mean? | They won by one goal | They ate cake after the match | *They lost very heavily | They played in the rain
exercise: «The wrecking ball pulverised the old school.» Which word is closest in meaning to pulverised? | Visited | Painted | Lifted | *Destroyed
test: «Mum pulverised the dried chilli in the bowl.» What is the best meaning? | Put in the fridge | *Ground into powder | Cooked in a hot pan | Taken out of the bowl
test: Which phrase would a science report use? | *pulverised rock | pulverised a song | pulverised with laughter | pulverised the weather
exam: Jayden reads «The team got pulverised» and says it means they were hurt in a crash. Is he right? | *No, it means they lost very heavily in a game | Yes, they were hurt in a crash | Yes, the team was painted bright colours | No, it means the team won easily
